{
    "background_story": "Davina, a 14th-century Scottish castle mistress, was renowned for her cunning and strategic mind. During the tumultuous Wars of Scottish Independence, she oversaw the fortification of her family's ancestral castle. Davina's most notable contribution was the design and implementation of a complex machicolation system, allowing defenders to drop objects or pour hot oil on attackers through openings in the ceiling of the gatehouse. Her innovative approach to castle defense proved invaluable during the siege of 1314, when English forces attempted to breach the castle walls. Davina's clever use of machicolation helped repel the invaders, securing a crucial victory for the Scottish forces. Her name became synonymous with clever tactics and castle design, earning her a place in the annals of Scottish history.",
